{"type": "result", "session_id": "local-bcd041f1530a14a39fc8ddaf7e835ea450ed0049fc480c38cf3ebe9f71ea9120", "exit_code": 0, "text": "All 6 tests pass. Let me verify the constraints were honored:\n- **Fixed the implementation, not the tests** — only `slugify.py` was edited; `test_slugify.py` is untouched (unchanged).\n- **No third-party dependencies** — `re` is part of the Python standard library, not a new dependency.\n- **Byte-for-byte test file** — I never wrote to `test_slugify.py`.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe original code built a list `out` where each separator character (`\" \"`, `\"-\"`, `\"_\"`) appended its own `\"-\"`. Joining with `\"\"` produced one dash *per* separator char, so `\"Hello   World\"` became `hello---world`. The collapse step `.replace(\"--\", \"-\")` used a **single** call that only replaced the first occurrence, leaving `hello--world`. That's why only `test_collapse_runs` failed.\n\n## The fix\n\nReplaced the loop with two `re.sub` passes (stdlib):\n1. `re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \" \", text.lower())` — drops everything non-alphanumeric and, crucially, turns each *run* of separators into a single space (so three spaces become one unit, not three).\n2. `re.sub(r\" +\", \"-\", cleaned).strip(\"-\")` — collapses any run of spaces into one dash and strips edge dashes.\n\nThis preserves the original behavior for all cases the other five tests cover (basic spaces, edge stripping, underscores, punctuation, empty), while correctly collapsing arbitrary separator runs.", "tokens": {"input": 214847, "output": 2237, "total": 217084, "cache_read": 210370, "cache_write": 0}, "duration_ms": 117661, "timestamp": 1793511334743}
